    "created_at": "2026-02-12T05:35:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-02-12T05:35:36Z",
    "body": "  > I think a strict reading of the spec supports current browser behavior.\n\n[§ 4.10.5.3 Common input element attributes](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#common-input-element-attributes) requires user agents to ignore `readonly` when it does not apply to the input element.\n\n  > When an attribute doesn't apply to an input element, **user agents must ignore the attribute, regardless of the requirements and definitions below**.\n\nIt does not say *how much* they must ignore it. The constraint validation added by the presence of `readonly` is one of the *definitions below*, whose section does not define much else.\n\nSo instead of rewording *do not apply*, perhaps this paragraph could just be extended by adding an exception for when consulting the presence of the attribute, rather than consulting whether it applies on the input type.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
